    "keywords": "Courage, gentle strength, integration, instinct, taming",
    "uprightMessage": "Real strength isn't suppressing fear — it's going forward while afraid. There is a lion inside you, and it carries every instinctive, primal feeling you have. You don't need to cage it, and you don't need to pretend it isn't there. Put a gentle hand on it and let it become your ally rather than your enemy.",
    "reversedReminder": "Do you feel flooded by your own fear? As if danger could arrive at any second, the body locked, unsure whether to fight or run. This freeze is your protection at work; it's guarding you. Don't rush to act — let yourself thaw slowly.",
    "symbolReading": "A woman gently opens the lion's mouth — not with force, but with love. The infinity sign above her head echoes the Magician: the same power in a different face. The Magician guides with will; Strength tames with tenderness. Her white robe is unclouded consciousness; the garland is her connection with nature. The lion's tail is tucked, not from fear but because it feels genuinely safe. That is strength: making another creature settled enough to put its guard down.",
    "jungianAnalysis": "This card shows what Jung called the integration of instinct — the animal side of us (fear, desire, anger) doesn't need destroying, it needs to be received with tenderness. In Albedo, consciousness begins learning to live alongside these primal forces. Rock Rose corresponds to extreme fear: when terror freezes everything, real strength is allowing yourself to be afraid, and finding one point of warmth in the very centre of it.",
    "flowerSpectrum": "When terror has frozen you in place and you can't move, Rock Rose keeps you company as the rhythm of breath returns inside the fear, and you begin, a little at a time, to thaw.",
    "step1Status": "You may have been through a shock — an accident, bad news, or a huge panic with no name. Your body remembers that moment; sometimes your heart suddenly races and you feel trapped.",
    "step2Allow": "I allow the fear to be here; it doesn't define me",
    "step3Body": "Notice your palms and the soles of your feet — do they go cold or tingle when fear arrives? Rub your hands until warm, then rest the warm palms on your cheeks.",
    "breathing": "In for three, hold for one, out extended to eight — with a soft \"ssss\" on the out-breath, like wind through leaves. That long exhale switches on your calming nerve.",
    "aromaGuide": "When fear comes, open the MUNI ONE bottle and take three deep breaths straight from it, letting smell be the anchor that draws you back into the present.",
    "dailyPractice": "Choose one small thing you've been avoiding out of fear — a phone call, walking into a shop, saying one sentence. Afterwards, press a hand to your heart for thirty seconds and tell yourself: I did it.",
    "awarenessNote": "Awareness is like a lighthouse in a storm. It can't stop the storm and doesn't need to. It simply stands steady and shines, reminding you: the weather will pass, and you are still here — you have been all along.",
