    "keywords": "Ending, transformation, release, honesty, rebirth",
    "uprightMessage": "Something is ending — a relationship, an identity, a way of living. Ending isn't failure; it's your soul clearing space for what comes next. You don't have to smile and pretend it's fine. Let yourself feel this loss honestly. Inside every real ending, a beginning is already folded.",
    "reversedReminder": "Have you been wearing the \"I'm fine\" mask, cheerful with everyone and enormously tired the moment you're alone? You're afraid of being seen fragile, so the real feeling gets pressed to the bottom. Wear a mask long enough and even you start forgetting the face underneath.",
    "symbolReading": "Death rides a white horse — white for purity, the cleansed power that comes through dying. The white rose on his black banner is the beauty that survives destruction. A king lies fallen at his feet: no outward identity escapes transformation. Between two distant towers the sun is rising or setting — ending and beginning at once. The river keeps flowing, reminding us that life never truly stops; it only changes form. The card is about shedding a skin, not about death.",
    "jungianAnalysis": "Death and rebirth is the most pivotal archetypal experience in individuation. Entering Citrinitas, the old ego structure begins to break down so a new Self can surface. The Agrimony person hides inner pain behind a cheerful mask — that mask has to die before the true self can be born. Jung called it the death of the Persona: painful, and the beginning of freedom.",
    "flowerSpectrum": "When you hide what you really feel behind a smile, afraid of anyone seeing your fragile side, Agrimony keeps you company as the mask comes off, and you find in honesty a peace deeper than pretending.",
    "step1Status": "To your friends you may be the one who lightens everything — laughing, joking, first to smooth a difficult moment. Privately you may be avoiding what you actually feel in all sorts of ways: bingeing shows till dawn, drinking, over-socialising. As long as you don't stop, you don't have to face it.",
    "step2Allow": "I allow myself not to smile, and let what's real be seen",
    "step3Body": "Notice your face — is there a small unconscious smile always at the corners of your mouth? Let your face relax completely, no expression at all, and feel the weight of a face without its makeup.",
    "breathing": "Breathing in, imagine drawing in honest air; breathing out, make a long \"ahh\", letting the sound carry away everything you've been holding up. Five times.",
    "aromaGuide": "After a shower, apply MUNI ONE oil to your chest while the body is most relaxed and least defended, and let the scent remind you: this is who you actually are.",
    "dailyPractice": "Find someone you trust today and tell them one honest thing you wouldn't normally say. It needn't be serious — \"actually I'm a bit tired today\" is enough. Practise honesty mattering more than cheerfulness.",
    "awarenessNote": "Awareness is like peeling an onion — under every mask there's another, and you don't have to finish in one go. Each time you're willing to let someone see a little more of you, that's enough. The core of you has been there all along, entirely intact.",
    "systemL1": "Agrimony",
