    "keywords": "Reconciling, purification, integration, the middle way, self-acceptance",
    "uprightMessage": "You are learning to bring the seemingly contradictory parts of your life together. You don't have to be perfect to deserve love. The parts you find not good enough — in your body, your character, your history — are still parts of you, and every one of them deserves tenderness. Reconciliation isn't fixing flaws; it's accepting the whole of yourself.",
    "reversedReminder": "Is there a part of yourself you're deeply uncomfortable with? A feature of your body, something in your past, a habit that won't shift. It feels unclean and you want it gone. That distaste itself is costing you more energy than the thing ever did.",
    "symbolReading": "The angel Michael stands with one foot in the water (the unconscious) and one on the shore (consciousness), pouring liquid between two cups — the water travels through the air without spilling, an image of perfect balance and integration. The square within the triangle on his chest is spirit set into matter. The crown on the distant mountains is the goal of individuation. The golden irises are new life after purification. The angel reminds you: the best alchemy isn't turning lead into gold, but seeing the gold that was in the lead all along.",
    "jungianAnalysis": "The alchemist was Jung's most beloved archetype — he read the whole of individuation as alchemy. Citrinitas is the drawing of light out of darkness. Crab Apple people are deeply sensitive to impurity, whether a physical blemish or a stain on the soul, and it unsettles them profoundly. Jung reminds us that the shadow isn't dirt to be scrubbed away but gold to be integrated. Real purification is acceptance, not removal.",
    "flowerSpectrum": "When you reject parts of yourself and feel something in body or mind is unclean and must be removed, Crab Apple keeps you company as acceptance replaces distaste, and you see that all of you is already whole.",
    "step1Status": "You may be deeply dissatisfied with your appearance, your body, or certain traits. You clean repeatedly, check repeatedly, or turn one flaw over and over in your mind. This isn't fastidiousness — it's a deep non-acceptance of yourself.",
