    "uprightMessage": "After the storm, the stars come out. You may have been through a great deal and stopped believing in much of anything. And just at your most tired, a thread of light appears — small, and entirely real. You don't have to become optimistic right away. Being willing to look up and see this one star is enough. Hope doesn't need to be loud; it only needs to be there.",
    "reversedReminder": "Have you given up on hope? Too many attempts, too many disappointments, and your heart has decided not to open again. \"It won't get better anyway\" has become the charm that protects you. But if you truly held no hope, you wouldn't have read this far — that thread of curiosity is the starlight.",
    "symbolReading": "A woman kneels naked at the water's edge, pouring one vessel back into the pool (nourishing the unconscious) and one onto the land, where it divides into five streams (the five senses returning). Eight stars stand above — the large yellow one at the centre is core hope; the seven smaller ones are the waking of the seven centres. She is naked because there is no longer anything to hide. The green tree and bird behind her mark vitality returning. There are no buildings, no structures — after the Tower falls, everything returns to its first beauty.",
    "jungianAnalysis": "The Star is what Jung called a glimpse of the Self — at the darkest point of individuation, the sudden sight of the possibility of wholeness. In the closing stage of Citrinitas, gold begins to appear in the dark. Gorse corresponds to losing hope after long hardship — not that you don't want things to improve, but that you no longer dare believe improvement is possible. Jung said the light of the Self doesn't need to be searched for; it comes through the very cracks where you are most fragile.",
    "flowerSpectrum": "When long disappointment has left you unable to hope and change feels impossible, Gorse keeps you company as a shoot pushes up through the soil of despair, giving one faint hope room to grow.",
    "step1Status": "You may have tried many things — seeing a doctor, changing jobs, ending a relationship — and nothing seems to have improved. You start to think perhaps this is simply your life now, with no surprises left. That isn't negativity; your heart is exhausted.",
    "step2Allow": "I allow a small hope back into my life",
    "step3Body": "Notice your ankles and calves — in long despair, the qi of the lower body stagnates easily. Stand and rise onto your toes, then lower, ten times, letting the blood move again.",
    "breathing": "Look up at the sky (or the ceiling). Breathing in, imagine starlight pouring into the crown of your head; breathing out, let it travel down the spine to the tailbone. Recharging your own body.",
