    "reversedReminder": "Do you often feel a fear with no object? You don't know what you're afraid of; you simply feel uneasy, as though something bad is coming. Nameless fear is the most punishing kind, because there's no enemy to face. But it is the language of your unconscious, and it's worth listening to.",
    "symbolReading": "The moon hangs in the sky as full moon and crescent at once — its light is reflected, not its own, so the images the unconscious returns may be distorted. Two dogs, one tame and one wild, howl beneath it: the two faces of instinct, domesticated habit and wild knowing. A crayfish crawls from the pool — the deepest unconscious content surfacing. Two towers are the gate to the unknown. The winding path has no end: in the moon's territory there are no straight lines, only turns.",
    "jungianAnalysis": "The Moon is what Jung called the deep Anima — fears and images older than personal memory. Entering Rubedo, the soul must cross the darkest reaches of the unconscious. Aspen's fear is the most primal kind: no object, no cause, a tremor that feels inherited in the DNA. Jung held that this innate fear connects to the oldest layer of the collective unconscious; to face it is to face the night of the whole human species.",
    "flowerSpectrum": "When a nameless unease pervades and you can't say where the fear comes from, Aspen keeps you company as inner safety returns in the fog, and the nameless slowly takes on an outline.",
    "step1Status": "You may wake at night sensing something in the room, feel your skin crawl on a street, or register an unexplained heaviness in the air. You know rationally there's nothing to fear, and your body reacts anyway. You don't say much about it, in case you're thought too sensitive.",
    "step2Allow": "I allow myself to feel what I can't name",
