    "keywords": "Inspiration, beginning, creative impulse, grounding the daydream, vitality budding",
    "uprightMessage": "A warm creative force is waking inside you, like the first flower of spring pushing through soil. It isn't asking you to do something great right now — it's inviting an honest question: that idea you keep carrying, are you willing to let it become slightly real?",
    "reversedReminder": "Plenty of inspiration and still no action — perhaps it isn't the wrong moment, but that you haven't yet given yourself permission to try. Start with the smallest possible step.",
    "symbolReading": "In the Ace of Wands, a hand reaches from a cloud holding a sprouting staff. The staff isn't a weapon but a symbol of life itself — it carries leaves and new buds, saying creativity is alive, organic, growing. The clouds are possibility not yet formed, and the appearance of the hand asks: inspiration has arrived; are you willing to take it?",
    "jungianAnalysis": "The energy of the Ace is a pure beginning, and in fire it shows as raw creative impulse. This is consciousness before differentiation, all possibility concentrated in a single point. Like the primordial image in psychology, it carries enormous potential waiting to be made concrete. The card reminds us that the source of every action is one simple impulse — to make, to light, to begin.",
    "flowerSpectrum": "The Clematis soul lives in dreams and finds reality not quite beautiful enough. This flower's gift is teaching us that the most beautiful dream can only truly open with both feet on the ground. Once the daydream has roots, imagination becomes creation.",
    "step1Status": "Something restless is stirring inside you and may still be living only in imagination. Notice whether you use \"later\" to avoid the very idea that excites you.",
    "step2Allow": "I allow inspiration to travel from dream into my hands",
    "step3Body": "Feel your palms; make a light fist and release it. That faint warmth is your creativity saying: I'm ready.",
    "breathing": "Breathe in for four, imagining a seed dropping into your palm; breathe out for six, imagining it beginning to sprout. Five times, feeling the warmth in your hands.",
    "aromaGuide": "Sweet orange with rosemary — the orange wakes your appetite for life, the rosemary helps turn inspiration into a first concrete step.",
    "dailyPractice": "Choose one small thing you've wanted to do and never started, and spend ten minutes on its first step. It doesn't need to be good; it only needs to begin. Treat those ten minutes as a gift to yourself.",
    "awarenessNote": "Inspiration is like a butterfly resting on your shoulder — the harder you grab, the faster it goes. But if you settle down and start making something small, you'll find it has quietly folded itself into what you're doing.",
    "systemL1": "Clematis",
