    "keywords": "Outlook, waiting, patience, the long voyage, sustained drive",
    "uprightMessage": "You've made the choice, the seed is in the ground, and now you wait for the ships to come back. Waiting isn't doing nothing — it's continuing to tend every seed you sowed on the days when nothing shows. Your effort is accumulating somewhere out of sight. Trust the process.",
    "reversedReminder": "If you feel a Monday-morning tiredness, it may not be the body but a lost sense of direction. Look back at why you set out. Is that reason still there?",
    "symbolReading": "The figure in the Three of Wands has his back to us, facing an open sea, three staffs planted firmly beside him. Ships are sailing in the distance. He has done what needed doing — chosen a direction, taken action — and now stands high and waits for an answer. The wisdom of this card is that real strength isn't only in starting; it's in how much steadiness and faith you can hold while you wait.",
    "jungianAnalysis": "The energy of the Three is creation and synthesis, and in fire it appears as the first harvest after initial action. Three is what one and two produce — the first concrete direction born of impulse meeting choice. On the journey of the psyche, three is \"set out but not arrived\". It tests your ability to hold faith mid-way, and whether you can keep going when nobody is applauding.",
    "flowerSpectrum": "Hornbeam's tiredness isn't real fatigue but a weariness with the ordinary — every day the same, no drive to begin. This flower brings back the capacity for momentum, so you can see the small differences and meaning inside repeating days.",
    "step1Status": "You may be in a waiting period — the work is done and the result hasn't shown. Notice whether you're starting to doubt the original decision, or whether that can't-get-going feeling is quietly surfacing.",
    "step2Allow": "I allow myself a gentle patience while I wait",
    "step3Body": "Rest a hand on your lower belly and feel the breath moving it. Like earth holding a seed, your body is quietly gathering strength.",
    "breathing": "Breathe in for five, imagining sunlight over a newly sown field; breathe out for five, imagining seeds swelling deep in the soil. Six times.",
    "aromaGuide": "Bergamot with juniper berry — the bergamot warmly settles the unease of waiting, the juniper gives steady drive to keep going.",
    "dailyPractice": "List three things you've done that haven't yet shown results, and write \"on its way\" beside each. Then do one small thing you can enjoy in the doing — make a pot of tea and drink it slowly.",
    "awarenessNote": "Waiting is like baking bread. Once the dough is in, opening the oven door to check keeps dropping the temperature. Better to trust that the heat is working and go do something you enjoy. The smell arrives when it's ready.",
