    "keywords": "Competition, friction, co-creation, sparking, many viewpoints",
    "uprightMessage": "You're in a noisy environment where everyone is putting their view forward, and it looks chaotic. Chaos isn't necessarily bad — when different sparks collide, the most unexpected light can appear. What matters is whether you can express yourself and still leave room for others.",
    "reversedReminder": "If conflict makes you angry or hurt, try to distinguish whether the feeling comes from the situation itself, or from somewhere deeper — a voice that says you aren't good enough.",
    "symbolReading": "Five figures each hold a staff in the Five of Wands, apparently in a brawl. Look closely, though: no one is actually hurt — it's more a heated discussion than a war. The crossing angles of the staffs suggest colliding perspectives, and every posture is full of energy. This card isn't about beating anyone; it's about finding, inside difference, a possibility better than anyone would have reached alone.",
    "jungianAnalysis": "The energy of the Five is conflict and breakthrough, and in fire it appears as several wills meeting head-on. Five breaks the stability of four and brings uncomfortable, necessary tension. Psychologically, five is the shadow's first appearance — what irritates us in someone else usually mirrors an unintegrated part of ourselves. The lesson of the Five is that your opponent in a contest is also your mirror.",
    "flowerSpectrum": "Holly is about the sharp feelings inside — envy, anger, a sense of unfairness. This flower doesn't ask you to press them down; it invites you to see that underneath the anger is a heart wanting to be loved and understood.",
    "step1Status": "You may be in disagreement with someone, or quietly competing. Notice whether it feels like people are aiming at you, or whether there's an imbalance inside you that can't be said. That feeling deserves to be seen gently.",
    "step2Allow": "I allow conflict to become the beginning of understanding",
    "step3Body": "Clench both fists for three seconds and feel the strength in your arms, then open your palms slowly. That passage from tight to loose is the body's memory of moving from resistance to acceptance.",
    "breathing": "Blow out three quick breaths through the mouth, as if blowing out candles, then take one slow deep breath in through the nose. Four rounds, giving the heat somewhere to go.",
    "aromaGuide": "Ylang-ylang with black pepper — the ylang-ylang softens the sharp feeling, the black pepper brings what has been pressed down to the surface where you can see it.",
    "dailyPractice": "If a disagreement arises today, silently think before answering: \"This person, like me, is doing their best to say something.\" Then respond however you choose, and notice whether it feels different.",
    "awarenessNote": "Conflict is like a forest in a storm. At the height of the wind the trees look ready to be uprooted. Afterwards you find that the deepest-rooted trees not only stood but grew stronger for resisting it. The point isn't to avoid the wind but to know your roots go deep enough.",
