    "keywords": "Speed, flow, messages, momentum, pace without hurry",
    "uprightMessage": "Things are accelerating, messages arriving, arrows of opportunity flying towards you. This is a moment full of momentum. Keep up with it, and don't let the speed carry you off. The best surfer isn't the fastest swimmer but the one who can meet the wave — soften the body and let the flow take you.",
    "reversedReminder": "If everyone seems too slow and the world can't keep up with you, that impatience may be costing you things only visible at a slower pace. Fast isn't automatically better.",
    "symbolReading": "The Eight of Wands is one of the most kinetic images in tarot — eight staffs cutting diagonally across the sky like arrows, like beams of light, like messages arriving all at once. There are no figures, because this energy is too fast to wait for anyone. The fields and river below suggest it's crossing open country. The card says: go with the momentum, and don't forget where you mean to land.",
    "jungianAnalysis": "The energy of the Eight is power reorganised and put to use, and in fire it appears as action at its highest efficiency. Eight is an infinity symbol — energy circulating without stopping. But speed is also a defence: some people use busyness to avoid feeling. Fire's eight invites you to tell the difference — is your speed flow, or escape? Real efficiency is heart and action in step.",
    "flowerSpectrum": "Impatiens people think fast, move fast, talk fast, and lose patience with other people's slowness. This flower's wisdom: your speed is a gift, and it only becomes strength once you learn not to isolate yourself with it. Slowing isn't compromise — it's letting others catch up.",
    "step1Status": "You may be in a fast stretch with a great deal happening at once. Notice whether the pace leaves no time to digest what any of it means, or whether other people's tempo is making you anxious.",
    "step2Allow": "I allow speed to be flow rather than flight",
    "step3Body": "Shake both hands quickly for ten seconds, then stop abruptly and feel the tingling in your fingertips. That instant between fast and still is your inner balance point.",
