    "keywords": "Resilience, holding on while tired, the last line, the wisdom of rest, a fighter's scars",
    "uprightMessage": "You've come a long way, carrying scars, and you're still standing. That resilience is one of your finest qualities. And here's a gentle question: are you continuing because you want to, or because stopping feels forbidden? Strength also needs to know when to set the shield down.",
    "reversedReminder": "If you're too tired to stand steadily and still won't rest, that isn't strength — it's harshness towards yourself. You deserve care, especially your own.",
    "symbolReading": "In the Nine of Wands a wounded figure with a bandaged head grips a staff before a row of others. His expression is tired but alert, an old soldier ready for the next round. The eight staffs behind him are every challenge already met. This card isn't about one more fight; it's about a genuinely brave decision — knowing you've worked hard enough, and allowing yourself to rest.",
    "jungianAnalysis": "The energy of the Nine is nearness to completion, and in fire it appears as the final testing of will. Nine is the last single digit, one step from wholeness — and because it's so close, both the temptation to quit and the pressure to persist peak here. Deep in the psyche, nine asks whether you can find a way of persisting that doesn't run on force. That's the turn from personal will to trusting life itself.",
    "flowerSpectrum": "Oak people are born fighters — never giving up, never showing weakness. Sometimes that strength becomes a cell: because they mustn't fall, every softness gets locked away. This flower teaches that real strength includes knowing when to rest, and that resting isn't quitting.",
    "step1Status": "You may have been holding something up for a long time, worn out in body and mind and still gritting your teeth. Notice whether \"I can manage\" has become an inability to stop rather than an honest report.",
    "step2Allow": "I allow the strong version of me to have soft moments too",
    "step3Body": "Find a wall and lean back, letting your whole back and the back of your head be supported. Feel what it's like not to hold yourself up, and stay there for a minute.",
    "breathing": "Breathing in, imagine strength pouring into your tired shoulders; breathing out, let the shoulders drop completely and give up all resistance. Ten times, looser with each exhale.",
    "aromaGuide": "Marjoram with sandalwood — the marjoram releases an over-braced body and mind, the sandalwood brings a deep settledness so relaxing feels safe.",
    "dailyPractice": "Finish your work or task thirty minutes early today and use that time for something purely caring — a bath, a walk, music, anything. Tell yourself: resting early isn't slacking; it's good sense.",
    "awarenessNote": "Resilience is like an elastic band. At full stretch its force is greatest, and held at full stretch it eventually snaps. The wisest use isn't testing how far it goes but letting it return to its natural length while it still has spring. So with you: the capacity to recover is worth more than the capacity to endure.",
    "systemL1": "Oak",
    "systemL2Echo": "VII",
    "systemEmotionGroup": "Despondency and despair",
    "systemMeridian": "Liver channel (jueyin; wood qi gone rigid)",
    "systemExtraordinary": "Du vessel (governing)",
    "systemAlchemy": "Citrinitas (the yellowing)",
    "systemQiFlow": "Wood too rigid to yield, spending itself out"
