    "uprightMessage": "You're carrying too much — some of it yours, some of it not. But you've always felt that if you don't carry it nobody will, so all the weight has landed on you. What you need now isn't more strength; it's permission. Not everything has to be finished by you.",
    "reversedReminder": "If you can barely breathe and are still taking things on, it's time for an honest question: are you indispensable, or do you need the feeling of being needed?",
    "symbolReading": "The Ten of Wands is a difficult image — someone bent under ten heavy staffs, walking slowly towards a distant house. He can't see the road because the staffs block his view. Each staff is a promise, a responsibility, someone's expectation. Look closely, though: nobody asked him to carry all of them at once. He could make two trips, ask for help, put some down — and he chose to carry the lot.",
    "jungianAnalysis": "The energy of the Ten is a cycle completed and saturated, and in fire it appears as creativity carried to its limit. Ten is one and zero — a return to the beginning, hinting the cycle is ending. Deep in the psyche, ten shows how much identity we've built on doing. The Ten of Wands' lesson: your worth isn't the weight you carry. Putting it down isn't failure — it clears space for the next cycle.",
    "flowerSpectrum": "Elm people are usually capable and reliable, the ones everyone leans on. When responsibility exceeds capacity they suddenly break — not from lack of strength, but from too much strength for too long. This flower's gift: even when you feel you can't hold it, that's just a signal, not a verdict on you.",
    "step1Status": "You may feel unable to breathe under responsibility, or believe that if you stop everything collapses. Notice whether \"I mustn't let anyone down\" is now costing you more than it protects.",
    "step2Allow": "I allow myself to set down the weight that isn't mine",
    "step3Body": "Standing, lift your shoulders as high as you can towards your ears, hold for five seconds, then let them fall abruptly. Three times, feeling the instant of relief.",
    "breathing": "Breathing in, feel the weight on your back; breathing out, imagine the burdens sliding one by one to the floor. Release one per exhale, until your back feels lighter.",
    "aromaGuide": "Cypress with sweet marjoram — the cypress helps you tell which responsibilities are genuinely yours, the marjoram loosens over-tight neck and shoulders so the body remembers relaxation.",
    "dailyPractice": "Write down everything currently on your plate, and mark each one: \"can only I do this?\" Find at least one thing you can hand over or set aside for now — and actually do it.",
    "awarenessNote": "Someone carrying too much is like a fruit tree with too much fruit — the branches bend and even break. Experienced growers thin the fruit in good time, not because the fruit is bad but so the tree lives longer and healthier. You are the tree. Caring for the trunk matters far more than one more fruit.",
