    "keywords": "Indifference, numbness, the offer in front of you, weariness, feeling again",
    "uprightMessage": "You sit and look at everything in front of you and nothing stirs. Not sad, not happy — just grey. This isn't your fault; hearts sometimes need a quiet stretch. But notice: life is holding something out to you. You may not see it right now, and it is genuinely there.",
    "reversedReminder": "If you've been numb a long time, it may be time for one small different thing — not to change anything, but to reboot your sensing system. Even taking a different road home.",
    "symbolReading": "In the Four of Cups someone sits under a tree, arms crossed, three cups already in front of him and unlooked at. A hand from a cloud offers a fourth, and he doesn't move. The tree is a state that is safe and closed. The three cups are the resources already there. The hand in the cloud is life's repeated invitation — not asking you to rally, just quietly asking whether you'd be willing to look.",
    "jungianAnalysis": "The energy of the Four is stability and structure, and in water it appears as feeling set solid and stalled. Four creates an enclosed space — safe and airless. It's a self-protection of the psyche: feeling too much hurt too much, so better to feel nothing. But numbness isn't peace; it's a freeze. Water's four asks: am I resting or avoiding? The first step back into feeling is usually admitting that you're numb.",
    "flowerSpectrum": "Wild Rose people have lost interest in living — anything will do, nothing much matters, like a fish holding still in water. Underneath that giving-up is a heart that once loved life very much. This flower wakes not passion but a quiet participation — the sense that being alive means something again.",
    "step1Status": "You may be in a state where nothing gets you going, unresponsive to the opportunities and invitations around you. That isn't laziness; it's your heart saying it needs to be understood. Notice whether \"anything's fine\" is how you avoid choosing.",
    "step2Allow": "I allow myself to wake slowly out of numbness",
    "step3Body": "Wash your hands in cold water for thirty seconds and feel the temperature. Dry them, and notice the faint alertness on your skin. Your body is still feeling.",
    "breathing": "Breathe gently and slowly, pausing two extra seconds at the top of each inhale. Inside that pause, notice what you can hear and what you can smell. Eight times.",
    "aromaGuide": "Wild orange with black spruce — the orange's brightness gives your senses a gentle shake, the spruce's forest note reminds you that the world outside is still waiting.",
    "dailyPractice": "Do one small thing you've never done today — walk into a shop you've never entered, taste a food you've never had, greet someone you'd normally pass. You don't have to like it; just let your sensing system take in a new signal.",
    "awarenessNote": "Numbness is like a frozen lake in winter — nothing appears to be happening, and beneath the ice the water is still moving. Your feelings haven't gone; they're held still. You don't need to break the ice by force. Just let a little warmth keep falling on it, and it will melt when it's ready.",
    "systemL1": "Wild Rose",
    "systemL2Echo": "X",
    "systemEmotionGroup": "Not present",
