    "keywords": "Loss, grief, what remains, turning around, letting go and seeing",
    "uprightMessage": "You've lost something precious, and that grief is real and important — don't rush past it. When you're ready, though, try turning around: behind you two cups are still standing, and there's a bridge to your future. What's gone won't come back, and what you still have deserves to be seen.",
    "reversedReminder": "If you keep living in the good of the past, it may not be because that memory was so perfect but because facing now takes courage. You can only live in this moment. The past is your root, not your home.",
    "symbolReading": "In the Five of Cups a figure in black looks down at three overturned cups, their contents already spilled. Behind him two cups still stand upright. Further off, a bridge leads to a building on the other bank. The card's most important detail is the direction of his gaze — he sees only what was lost, with his back to what remains. This isn't a card about loss; it's a card about where you choose to look.",
    "jungianAnalysis": "The energy of the Five is imbalance and breakthrough, and in water it appears as loss and grief. Five breaks the numbness of four, waking feeling through pain. Deep in the psyche, loss is necessary — it removes what you thought you couldn't be without and forces the question: without these, are you still you? Water's five is among the most painful cards, and the most honest: the pain is proof that you loved deeply.",
    "flowerSpectrum": "Honeysuckle people live in longing for the past — a former love, a former time, a former version of themselves. This flower doesn't ask you to forget; it helps you carry those good memories forward into now. The past is treasure you carry, not a direction you retreat in.",
    "step1Status": "You may be inside a feeling of loss, or returning again and again to a period behind you. Notice whether memory is helping you avoid something in the present, or whether you see only what you lack and miss what you still hold.",
    "step2Allow": "I allow grief to move through, then turn and see what remains",
    "step3Body": "Close your eyes and turn slowly in a full circle, feeling your body whole in the space. Back where you started, open your eyes and see what's in front of you as if for the first time.",
    "breathing": "Breathing in, think of one thing you've lost and say thank you silently. Breathing out, think of one thing you still have and smile inwardly. Alternate five times.",
    "aromaGuide": "Bitter orange with myrrh — the orange brings a warm consolation so grief has somewhere to go, the myrrh helps you find transformation inside loss rather than sinking into it.",
    "dailyPractice": "Draw two columns on a piece of paper: on the left, three things you've lost or miss; on the right, three things you have now. Don't compare them — just look at them side by side and let both be true.",
    "awarenessNote": "Grief is like a river running through your heart. Don't let it flow and it becomes a widening lake, taking more and more room. Let it pass and the banks grow new things from the watering. Let grief move through; don't let it move in.",
    "systemL1": "Honeysuckle",
