    "keywords": "Childhood, patterns, memory, innocence, waking from an old script",
    "uprightMessage": "Memories from childhood or old patterns are surfacing — a person, a feeling, a scene you thought long forgotten. They aren't here to send you back but to let you see clearly: some of the ways you respond today were planted a very long time ago. Seeing is the first step of change.",
    "reversedReminder": "If you notice yourself meeting similar problems in different situations, it may be time to look at the repeating pattern. It isn't bad luck — you simply haven't yet seen the old program that keeps running.",
    "symbolReading": "The Six of Cups has a fairytale colour — an older child hands a flower-filled cup to a smaller one, with five more flowering cups around them, in an old courtyard with a safe, nostalgic atmosphere. The deeper meaning isn't only \"happy memories\" but \"what patterns did you bring from childhood?\" Some of the flowers in those cups may already have withered.",
    "jungianAnalysis": "The energy of the Six is harmony and return, and in water it appears as revisiting past emotional experience. Six is the number of memory, taking you back to when the psyche was first shaped. Jung said that before consciousness has woken, the unconscious is already directing your life. Water's six invites you back to those earliest emotional imprints — not to sink into them, but to see clearly which choices are yours and which are an old script repeating.",
    "flowerSpectrum": "Chestnut Bud stands for the pattern of repeating the same mistake — not for want of intelligence, but because you moved on before drawing the wisdom out of the experience. This flower helps you press pause, look back at what the lesson actually was, and set out again carrying it.",
    "step1Status": "You may be feeling a strong sense of familiarity — a person or a situation reminding you of the past. Notice whether you're meeting an adult problem with a childhood strategy. That response once protected you; it may not fit now.",
    "step2Allow": "I allow myself to see the old pattern and let go of it gently",
    "step3Body": "Place a hand three fingers below your navel, where you were first connected in your mother's body. Press gently and feel the warmth and pulse there.",
    "breathing": "Breathing in, recall a happy childhood scene; breathing out, return to this moment. Six times, arriving here more completely each time.",
    "aromaGuide": "Sweet orange with Roman chamomile — the orange connects to childhood warmth, the chamomile comforts the small self still living inside you.",
    "dailyPractice": "Spend ten minutes writing down a pattern that recurs in your relationships (\"I always apologise first\", \"I always avoid conflict\"). Then ask: when did it start? How old were you when you learned it? Look at that age of yourself with understanding.",
    "awarenessNote": "Every one of us has a younger self living inside, still meeting the world with what they learned back then. Like a computer running a program installed ten years ago — the program isn't faulty, it simply can't keep up with what's needed now. You don't have to delete it. Just see it, understand it, and decide whether to upgrade.",
    "systemL1": "Chestnut Bud",
    "systemL2Echo": "Page of Cups",
