    "keywords": "Romance, quest, idealisation, the emissary of feeling, hesitation before setting out",
    "uprightMessage": "You're the one who'll cross mountains for a beautiful feeling. The cup in your hand never spills, because you know feeling is the most precious thing there is. You move through ordinary days poetically, finding romance in the plain, and that sensibility makes you the most tender landscape in someone else's life.",
    "reversedReminder": "Have you walked that road many times in your mind, without taking a single step in reality? When \"I'll start tomorrow\" becomes a daily sentence, it may not be that you don't want it enough, but that it's been too long since you let your body move first. However steady the cup is held, the horse still has to walk.",
    "symbolReading": "The knight rides slowly forward, the cup raised in his hand without a drop spilled — feeling treasured and attended to. Fish and wings are worked into his armour; fire within water gives him both depth of feeling and the capacity to act. A winding river lies ahead, hinting that the journey of feeling has no straight line and every bend is a story. The white horse walks calmly, nothing like the Knight of Wands' gallop.",
    "jungianAnalysis": "The romantic knight carries an eternal search for beauty and love; he is poet and messenger both. His shadow is Hornbeam's hesitation — the heart already on the road while the body stays at the starting line, postponing the journey indefinitely with \"not ready yet\". Individuation invites him to see that a knight doesn't set out once fully prepared; he becomes prepared, slowly, after setting out.",
    "flowerSpectrum": "When you know the direction but your body can't summon the energy, when every morning feels like a Monday and what you want to do keeps sliding to tomorrow, Hornbeam keeps you company as you recover that little freshness before setting out, so the first step feels less heavy.",
    "step1Status": "You may already know exactly where you want to go — that person, that thing, that journey — yet every morning you wake feeling you don't have the strength to set out. It isn't that you don't want to; you just can't summon the energy, like a Monday-morning tiredness.",
    "step2Allow": "I allow myself to take one small step while still feeling unready.",
    "step3Body": "Notice the front of your thighs and your knees — when you want to set out but keep not moving, this is where it often feels heavy. Stand up and gently lift your knees in place ten times, letting your legs remember what walking feels like.",
    "breathing": "In for four, imagining the cool of early morning pouring into your chest; out for four, gently letting go of \"later, maybe\". On the third out-breath let your shoulders drop. Six times, until your body wants to move before your mind does.",
    "aromaGuide": "Apply MUNI ONE oil to your temples and the back of your neck, as if opening a window for yourself — not to push you out the door, but to let today's air in first, until you naturally want to follow it.",
    "dailyPractice": "Today choose one small thing you've wanted to do for a long time and never started, and do only the first five minutes — write the first sentence, make the first call, walk to the door. After five minutes you may stop, but most of the time the horse is already walking.",
    "awarenessNote": "Awareness is like watching a horse standing at the river's edge — it isn't that it doesn't want to cross; it's waiting for the water to settle a little. You don't have to urge it on. Just notice that its ears have already turned toward the far bank: that is the sign of setting out.",
    "systemL1": "Hornbeam",
