    "keywords": "Blindfolded, stalemate, fear of the unknown, refusing to look, facing and accepting",
    "uprightMessage": "You sit blindfolded, two swords held in a fragile balance. Behind you is the sea you daren't look at — the unknown that frightens you. But you can't keep your eyes shut forever. Those fears you can't name, if you're willing to face them, usually turn out far smaller than you imagined.",
    "reversedReminder": "If that nameless unease has been with you a long time, it may be time to ask: what am I actually afraid of? Sometimes just saying or writing the fear takes half its force away.",
    "symbolReading": "The Two of Swords is spare and strong — a blindfolded figure sits on a stone bench by the sea, arms crossed with a sword in each hand. The blindfold is deliberate avoidance: not unable to see, but unwilling. The crossed swords make a defensive posture. Behind, a crescent moon and scattered rocks hint at emotional currents in the unconscious. The message: what you think doesn't exist because you won't look at it has been waiting behind you all along.",
    "jungianAnalysis": "The energy of the Two is opposition and choice, and in air it appears as reason locked in a standoff with the unknown. The two-mind divides the world into known and unknown, safe and dangerous, then chooses to stay on the known side. That avoidance has a price — the energy spent maintaining the blindfold would be enough to face what you fear. Air's two invites you to understand that uncertainty isn't a threat; it's the precondition for every possibility.",
    "flowerSpectrum": "Aspen's fear has no clear object — not the specific fear of snakes or heights, but a diffuse insecurity, as if something bad could happen at any time. This flower's gift is an inner safety: even without knowing what's in the dark, your light is enough for the path at your feet.",
    "step1Status": "You may be feeling an unease or anxiety you can't explain, with no cause to point to. Or you've chosen not to think about a decision. Notice whether busyness, entertainment or denial is how you avoid that faint fear.",
    "step2Allow": "I allow myself to open my eyes slowly to the unknown sea",
    "step3Body": "Close your eyes and rest your hands palm-down on your thighs. Feel the chair beneath you and the floor below that. You are steady, even unseeing. Open your eyes slowly, letting the light in a little at a time.",
    "breathing": "Breathing in, imagine yourself inside a safe bubble; breathing out, let the bubble expand slowly to include the whole room. Six times, letting safety widen.",
    "aromaGuide": "Roman chamomile with patchouli — the chamomile soothes nameless fear with warmth, the patchouli plants your feet firmly on the ground.",
    "dailyPractice": "Take a piece of paper and try to write down what has felt unsettling lately without an explanation. Even \"I don't know why but I'm afraid\" is fine. Read it aloud to yourself and notice the words becoming less frightening.",
    "awarenessNote": "Fear of the unknown is like a strange noise at night — the longer you don't look, the worse it gets, and imagination inflates it into anything. When you finally get up to check, it's usually the wind in the curtain. Fear draws its power from your avoidance, not from the thing itself.",
