    "keywords": "Heartbreak, betrayal, the wisdom in pain, honest tears, releasing resentment",
    "uprightMessage": "Your heart hurts — perhaps someone you trusted wounded you, perhaps a cruel truth arrived. This pain is real; don't pretend it isn't. And after you've let yourself feel it completely, look again: once the sword is drawn out, the wound it leaves eventually becomes the place the light gets in.",
    "reversedReminder": "If you keep retelling the story of how you were hurt, what you may need isn't more sympathy but a quiet space where the wound can start to close. Gripping resentment is like holding a hot coal — the burn is yours.",
    "symbolReading": "The Three of Swords is one of tarot's most direct images — three blades through a red heart, against grey storm and pouring rain. No figure appears, because this pain is so pure and immediate it needs no story. The three swords are three sources of hurt: another's betrayal, the cruelty of truth, and your own part in it. The rain is cleansing tears — grief needs an outlet, and tears are the heart breathing.",
    "jungianAnalysis": "The energy of the Three is creation and expression, and in air it appears as painful awakening through colliding thought. Three is the piercing force produced when truth (the first sword) meets trust (the second) plus reality (the third). Deep in the psyche, every heartbreak is an expansion of consciousness — the gap between the world you assumed and the world that is has been forced open. It hurts, and afterwards you see more clearly.",
    "flowerSpectrum": "Willow people carry resentment and a sense of injustice — \"why me? this isn't fair.\" That victim feeling is real, and living inside it turns it into a cell of your own making. Willow's wisdom carries you from \"why me\" towards \"since it happened, what can I take with me as I go on\".",
    "step1Status": "You may be in heartbreak or deep disappointment, or carrying a story about unfairness. Notice whether resentment is protecting you from being hurt again while also preventing you from actually healing.",
    "step2Allow": "I allow heartbreak to bring wisdom rather than let resentment take root",
