    "keywords": "Quick thinking, straight at the target, a storm of mind, incisiveness, ceaseless turning",
    "uprightMessage": "Your thinking moves like a gale, cutting to the heart of a problem faster than anyone. While others are still deciding whether to act, you've swung and finished. That quickness makes you the most reliable person when speed matters. Just remember — sometimes slow thinking is closer to the truth than fast.",
    "reversedReminder": "Can't stop the wheel turning? The same conversation replayed thirty times, the same question analysed from every side with no conclusion. That isn't thinking; it's idling. What you need may not be more thought but a stretch of not thinking at all.",
    "symbolReading": "The knight charges, sword raised overhead, into churning cloud. All four hooves are off the ground; fire within air takes speed to its limit. Butterflies and birds are embroidered on his cloak — thoughts in flight. Look closely at his expression, though: not resolve but urgency. This isn't a purposeful charge; it looks more like being chased by his own thinking.",
    "jungianAnalysis": "The charging mind takes pride in mental speed, able to construct an intricate argument in an instant. Its shadow is White Chestnut's whirlwind — the brain becomes a washing machine that never stops turning and never gets anything clean. The challenge of individuation is learning to press pause, returning the mind's blade to its sheath and finding clarity in quiet.",
    "flowerSpectrum": "When thoughts spin like a tornado and the voice in your head wakes you at night, White Chestnut keeps you company as the over-revved engine drops to idle, and clear thinking returns in the quiet.",
    "step1Status": "Your head may be humming with a thought that recurs however hard you try to stop it — the more you try to control it, the faster it turns, your body in bed and your mind at full speed.",
    "step2Allow": "I allow my head to go blank for now; not every thought needs handling.",
    "step3Body": "Notice your temples. Overworked thinking can leave them full or tight — press small circles there with your index fingers, like pressing a slow-down key for the brain.",
    "breathing": "In for three, bringing attention from the crown down to the belly; out for seven, imagining every thought drifting to the ground like a falling leaf — not chased, not picked up, not sorted. Eight times.",
    "aromaGuide": "Apply MUNI ONE oil to your temples and the hollows at the base of the skull, letting the scent be a pair of gentle hands steadying that spinning wheel.",
    "dailyPractice": "Spend ten minutes today on something purely manual — knead dough, fold laundry, wipe a table. When thought tries to join in, bring attention back to your fingertips and let the body lead the mind into rest.",
    "awarenessNote": "Awareness is standing beside the waterfall rather than in it — the water still crashes down and you're simply watching, not required to jump in. You'll find that when you stop trying to stop it, it quietens on its own.",
    "systemL1": "White Chestnut",
    "systemL2Echo": "9 of Swords",
