    "keywords": "Clear sight, independence, wisdom earned through grief, boundaries, insight",
    "uprightMessage": "You are someone who has come through the storm, and afterwards you didn't turn fragile — you turned clear. Your sword was ground by experience, every notch a lesson. You know that setting a boundary isn't coldness but self-respect, and that telling the truth isn't cruelty but regard. This tempered strength is the gentlest kind.",
    "reversedReminder": "Have thoughts like \"it's everyone else's fault\" and \"life has been unfair to me\" been living in you too long? The hurt was real, and locking yourself into the role of the wronged also locks the key out of reach.",
    "symbolReading": "The Queen sits above the clouds, one hand raising the sword and the other extended slightly, as if drawing an invisible boundary. Her veil half covers her face — grief not suppressed but gently shaded. Willows in wind are carved into the throne; water meeting air gives her reason with deep feeling inside it. The sky is grey-blue, clearing after rain.",
    "jungianAnalysis": "The queen of grief is the wise one who gained insight through suffering, her clarity assembled after heartbreak. Her shadow is Willow's resentment — making misfortune an identity and building walls out of \"what I've suffered\". The breakthrough of individuation is admitting the hurt is real while refusing to let it be the only story that defines you.",
    "flowerSpectrum": "When you feel the world owes you, chew over unfair memories, and look at life with a faint resentment, Willow keeps you company as the gripped grievance loosens and the pen for writing your own story comes back to your hand.",
    "step1Status": "You may be regarding everything with a \"none of this is my fault\" feeling, stung slightly by other people's luck, carrying an unspoken indignation about your own situation.",
    "step2Allow": "I allow life to contain unfairness, and allow myself to stop carrying it.",
    "step3Body": "Notice your jaw and the corners of your mouth. Grievance often draws the mouth down and clenches the teeth — open your mouth slightly and let the jaw hang, releasing the tension there.",
    "breathing": "In for four, imagining rain washing across your face; out for six, letting the water take the sourness from your eyes and the bitterness from your mouth. Each round rinses a little. Seven times.",
    "aromaGuide": "Apply MUNI ONE oil to the backs of your hands and inner wrists, and when the grievance rises, breathe it in deeply — let that warmth remind you that you deserve a better story.",
    "dailyPractice": "Find one thing today you've always felt was unfair and write it down. Underneath, write: \"This happened, and now I can choose ___\" — fill it with the first thing that comes to mind.",
    "awarenessNote": "Awareness is standing at a window in the rain watching the drops run down the glass — each takes a different path, some fast, some slow, and none of them is wrong. Grievance is like that. Let it slide; it doesn't need you to keep holding it.",
