    "keywords": "Reason, fairness, authoritative judgement, clear thinking, the judge hardest on himself",
    "uprightMessage": "Your thinking is a scalpel, opening the surface of things precisely to reach what's underneath. You trust logic and value order, and in chaos you're the one who can analyse calmly and give a direction. That clarity is a rare gift, making you the most dependable judgement in a complicated situation.",
    "reversedReminder": "Are you running a trial of yourself that can never reach the standard? Thoughts of \"I should have, I shouldn't have, it's all on me\" may not be pursuit of excellence but guilt used as punishment. The first rule of fairness is to be fair to yourself too.",
    "symbolReading": "The King sits upright, sword held vertically, the light from the blade an image of absolute clarity and truth. His expression is serious rather than cold. Butterflies and crescent moons are carved on the throne; air within air gives his judgement its transparency. His robe carries fine geometric patterning, every line perfectly symmetrical — a hint of his exacting pursuit of order.",
    "jungianAnalysis": "The supreme judge is the summit of mental development, able to light the darkest corner with thought. His shadow is Pine's self-judgement — all that clear discernment turned inward, becoming a court that can never close its case. The final challenge of individuation is to apply fairness to himself as well: a truly just judge does not sentence himself more harshly than anyone else.",
    "flowerSpectrum": "When you blame yourself too much, shoulder other people's mistakes, and feel it is \"still not enough\" however well you do, Pine keeps you company as you step down from your own trial, seeing that you have done your best and deserve your own forgiveness.",
    "step1Status": "You may be blaming yourself over and over for something already past — even when everyone else says it wasn't your fault. Your verdicts on yourself are always harsher than on anyone else, and \"I should have known\" has repeated in your mind many times.",
    "step2Allow": "I allow myself to set down the responsibility that isn't mine; the case can be closed.",
    "step3Body": "Notice your upper back, between the shoulder blades — in self-blame it is often tight and drawn inward. Clasp your hands gently behind your back, draw the shoulder blades toward each other, hold for three seconds and release, as if undoing an invisible set of shackles.",
    "breathing": "In for four, out for six, and on each out-breath say once, inside: \"This one can be let go.\" Seven times, letting each out-breath run a little longer than the in-breath — a longer exhale is the body's version of absolution.",
    "aromaGuide": "Apply MUNI ONE oil to the inside of your wrists and your chest, then rest both hands, one over the other, on your chest for a moment. This gesture is neither trial nor defence; it lends the judge's seat, for a while, to someone willing to be gentle with you.",
    "dailyPractice": "Today find one small thing you keep blaming yourself for, write it as a single sentence, and after it write: \"I did it the way I knew how at the time.\" Read it once, then fold the paper. Closing a case needs no one else's signature.",
    "awarenessNote": "Awareness is like sitting in the public gallery of a courtroom — watching that judge retry the same case again and again, and the defendant every time is you. You don't have to overturn the verdict. Just notice: the judge and the defendant are the same person, and he may choose to adjourn.",
    "systemL1": "Pine",
    "systemL2Echo": "9 of Cups",
    "systemEmotionGroup": "Despondency and despair",
    "systemMeridian": "Large Intestine channel (yangming; what should be let go is not fully released)",
    "systemExtraordinary": "Du vessel (governing)",
    "systemAlchemy": "Rubedo (the reddening)",
    "systemQiFlow": "Yangming not descending; the turbid accumulates within"
