    "keywords": "Lack, hardship, worry for loved ones, the lit window, drowning in worry",
    "uprightMessage": "You're walking a stretch that looks hard — financial pressure, or some form of lack. Your mind may not be on yourself alone but on the people you love. Have you noticed the lit window, though? Help has been there all along; you've been walking head-down through the snow and missed it. Look up.",
    "reversedReminder": "If all your worry goes to other people — your children, your parents, your partner — that worry may be consuming the energy you need for yourself. You have to steady yourself before you can really help anyone.",
    "symbolReading": "The Five of Pentacles is a scene in deep winter — two ragged figures walk through snow past a church's stained-glass window. The window shows five coins, the warmth and resources inside. Heads down, they don't see it. One uses a crutch, one is barefoot: different degrees of difficulty. The crucial detail is that the door is open and they haven't gone in. The hardship is real, and so is the help.",
    "jungianAnalysis": "The energy of the Five is crisis and breakthrough, and in earth it appears as material security giving way. Five breaks the closure of four, and this breaking is painful — what you gripped has been taken by force. Deep in the psyche, the Five of Pentacles is scarcity consciousness at its peak: not that you truly have nothing, but that fear makes it seem so. When worry takes all your attention, you can't see the support already approaching.",
    "flowerSpectrum": "Red Chestnut people treat worry as an expression of love — over-worry about a child's safety, a partner's health, a family's future. That worry doesn't help them and drains you. Red Chestnut's wisdom is turning worry into trust — trusting that the people you love have their own strength for what they face.",
    "step1Status": "You may be experiencing some form of scarcity or worry — material or emotional. Notice how much of the worry is about you and how much about people you love. Over-worrying about others is sometimes a way of avoiding your own situation.",
    "step2Allow": "I allow myself to look up in the snow and see the lit window",
    "step3Body": "Put your hands over your kidneys (either side of the lower back) and let the warmth of your palms soak in. The kidneys are where fear lives; warming them settles your deepest unease.",
    "breathing": "Breathing in, imagine your feet on warm ground rather than cold snow; breathing out, let that warmth rise slowly from your soles through the whole body. Eight times.",
    "aromaGuide": "Ginger with cinnamon — the ginger warms the core that fear has cooled and brings solidity, the cinnamon is like a hot drink reminding you that you're less alone than you thought.",
    "dailyPractice": "Take an honest inventory today — list on paper the resources you actually have: a place to stay, food you can eat, someone you can call. Then read the list and let yourself feel it: you are richer than your worry has been telling you.",
    "awarenessNote": "Worry is like tinted glasses — put them on and the whole world turns grey. What you see of the difficulty is real, and so is the warmth you can't see. The problem isn't that your world is too grey; it's that you've forgotten you can take the glasses off. Try it — you may find that the lit window is one turn of the head away.",
    "systemL1": "Red Chestnut",
