    "keywords": "Giving, receiving, generosity, hope, resources in motion",
    "uprightMessage": "Perhaps you're the one giving right now, perhaps the one receiving — either position has dignity. If you're giving, give without superiority. If you're receiving, receive without shame. Resources move like water; today you're upstream and tomorrow you may be down. These are natural cycles.",
    "reversedReminder": "If hardship has become so familiar that hope has gone — \"it won't get better anyway\" — that despair may limit you more than your actual circumstances. Hope doesn't need evidence; it only needs your willingness to believe.",
    "symbolReading": "In the Six of Pentacles a richly dressed figure holds a set of scales and distributes coins to two kneeling people. The scales mean fairness — giving is allocation according to need, not charity. Six coins are placed at various points, resources in motion. The deepest meaning isn't about money but the exchange of energy: when you let your resources move generously, the whole system benefits, yourself included.",
    "jungianAnalysis": "The energy of the Six is harmony and repair, and in earth it appears as balance restored between giving and receiving in material life. After the lack of five, six brings relief. But earth's six carries a subtle task: your attitude to giving and receiving reflects your relationship with your own worth. Do you find receiving help weak? Do you confirm your value through giving? Six invites you to let resources flow without attaching judgement.",
    "flowerSpectrum": "Gorse people have given up hope — so many methods tried and none of them worked, so they no longer believe things will improve. What separates them from those who have genuinely quit is that they still try, only without expectation. Gorse rekindles the extinguished spark — not blind optimism, but trust founded on the nature of life.",
    "step1Status": "You may be receiving help of some kind and feeling uncomfortable, or giving with a hidden expectation attached. Perhaps you've grown discouraged about anything improving. Notice how the word \"hope\" feels — if it seems false, that's Gorse territory.",
    "step2Allow": "I allow hope to bud again in someone else's kindness",
    "step3Body": "When someone hands you something — a glass of water, a meal, a parcel — receive it with both hands. Feel the respect and openness in that gesture. You deserve to receive life's offerings with both hands.",
    "breathing": "Breathing in, imagine your palms open upwards, ready to receive; breathing out, imagine them turning over, ready to give. Six times, feeling giving and receiving as two faces of one movement.",
    "aromaGuide": "Wild orange with frankincense — the orange brings a bright hopefulness like a sweet of sunlight in winter, the frankincense connects you to a larger trust so you know you aren't alone.",
    "dailyPractice": "Do one anonymous kindness today — hold a door for a stranger, pay for the next customer's coffee, pick up litter in a park. Don't let anyone know it was you. Feel the pure pleasure of giving that asks nothing back.",
    "awarenessNote": "Hope is like a seed in frozen ground — you can't see what it's doing, and it knows. It's waiting, not passively but gathering strength quietly under the ice. Hope may seem naïve or impractical, and then look at spring: every flower was once a seed buried in lightless dark. Your hope is working under the ice too.",
    "systemL1": "Gorse",
