    "keywords": "Waiting for harvest, assessment, patience, small setbacks, long investment",
    "uprightMessage": "You've worked this field a long time and can finally stop and look at what grew. Some fruit is better than expected, some worse — that's a real harvest. This isn't the moment to give up, and it's a good moment for honest assessment: what's worth continuing? What needs adjusting? Patience isn't waiting blindly; it's waiting with awareness.",
    "reversedReminder": "If one small failure makes you want to abandon the whole field, the discouragement isn't because the failure was large but because your expectation of yourself is high. One thin harvest doesn't mean you aren't a good farmer.",
    "symbolReading": "In the Seven of Pentacles a farmer leans on his hoe, studying a plant heavy with seven coins. His expression is neither delight nor disappointment but assessment — he's weighing whether this is worth continued investment. The ground at his feet is the labour already given. The essential element is the act of stopping to look: not working, not quitting, but reflecting. That's the difference between the wise farmer and the impulsive one.",
    "jungianAnalysis": "The energy of the Seven is inner testing and refinement, and in earth it appears as confidence in a long investment being tested. Seven is a touchstone — when you've given much and see only a little, will you continue? Earth's seven isn't dramatic like fire's; its test is quiet, long, day after day. Psychologically it asks whether you can keep doing the right thing without immediate feedback.",
    "flowerSpectrum": "Gentian people deflate easily at setbacks — not total despair but a sagging \"here we go again\". A few marks short in an exam, a small hitch in a plan, slower progress than expected — these small failures make them want to stop. Gentian brings resilience in the face of setbacks: a step back isn't falling; it's how you go forward better.",
    "step1Status": "You may be mid-way through a long project and doubting whether the investment is worth it. Or a small setback has cost you confidence in the whole thing. Notice whether you're using one failure to negate all the effort.",
    "step2Allow": "I allow small setbacks to be signals for adjustment rather than reasons to quit",
    "step3Body": "Find a tree or a potted plant and put your hand on its trunk or soil. Feel that quiet vitality — it grows only a little each day and never stops for that.",
    "breathing": "In for six, for your tending; hold for three, for the waiting; out for six, for your trust. Seven times, letting the rhythm tell your body that giving and harvest are separated by time.",
    "aromaGuide": "Patchouli with rosemary — the patchouli brings earthlike steadiness so a small setback doesn't shake you, the rosemary lifts the spirit for going on.",
    "dailyPractice": "Look back today at a project that's \"behind expectations\" and find at least three small things already achieved, however small. Then adjust one aspect you think could be done better. That's patience with awareness.",
    "awarenessNote": "A bamboo shows almost no growth for four years, then can rise twenty metres in six weeks in its fifth. Those four years weren't idleness but rooting — the part you couldn't see is exactly what made the explosion possible. Your effort is the same: seeing no progress doesn't mean nothing is happening. You may be in your rooting years.",
    "systemL1": "Gentian",
