    "keywords": "Refinement, focus, vocation, repeated practice, finding your direction",
    "uprightMessage": "You're getting better at something — you may not feel it yet, and your craft is refining with every repetition. This isn't talent but attention: you're willing to repeat the same movement until it becomes second nature. In that repetition, you're also moving closer to your real vocation.",
    "reversedReminder": "If you can't find the thing to focus on and keep sampling many fields shallowly, the problem may not be a lack of direction but a fear of committing fully and finding it was the wrong one. Only real commitment can tell you.",
    "symbolReading": "In the Eight of Pentacles a craftsman sits at his bench carving a coin, six finished pieces already hung on the wall, a seventh in his hands, an eighth waiting. He's away from the city, having chosen focus over noise. Each coin is finer than the last — continuous improvement. The core message: masters aren't born; they're practised.",
    "jungianAnalysis": "The energy of the Eight is power integrated and applied, and in earth it appears as finding your calling through repeated practice. Eight is a number about mastery — not knowing many things but doing one thing to its limit. In individuation, the Eight of Pentacles corresponds to finding your place in the world: the thing you do with both focus and satisfaction. Not necessarily the most glamorous, certainly the truest. Wild Oat's question is answered here: a vocation isn't found — it's practised into being.",
    "flowerSpectrum": "Wild Oat people are gifted and directionless, wanting to try everything and finding nothing sufficient. Inside there's an anxiety: \"I came here to do something important and I can't find it.\" Wild Oat doesn't give you the direction; it helps you grow quiet enough to feel — among everything you've tried, which one made you forget time? That's the clue.",
    "step1Status": "You may be in a stage that requires a lot of repetition and finding it dull, or still moving between directions without knowing where to put your energy. Notice whether \"I haven't found my calling\" is how you avoid sitting down and practising properly.",
    "step2Allow": "I allow myself to meet who I really am through repeated practice",
    "step3Body": "Choose one simple hand movement — folding a sheet of paper, peeling an apple — and do it once very slowly. Feel each fine muscular action. Focus is itself a bodily pleasure.",
    "breathing": "Breathing in, imagine gathering all your attention to a single point; breathing out, send that focus into your hands. Five times, then take that focus into the work in front of you.",
    "aromaGuide": "Rosemary with lemon — rosemary is the guardian of concentration, keeping you awake inside repetition; lemon brings a freshness so practice doesn't become drudgery.",
    "dailyPractice": "Choose one skill you're learning, however small, and give it thirty fully attentive minutes today. Phone off, music off — only you and the thing. Afterwards, note one small improvement you noticed.",
    "awarenessNote": "A vocation doesn't arrive like lightning announcing \"this is it\". It's more that as you walk a road, the ground grows more comfortable and the view more agreeable, and one day you look back and find you've come a long way. Don't wait for the lightning. Start walking. You meet your calling on the road, not standing still waiting for it to find you.",
    "systemL1": "Wild Oat",
